  "plain": "In 1947, at the Roswell crash site in New Mexico, debris was found that was thin as foil yet strange, according to Jesse Marcel Sr., the Army Air Force intelligence officer who says he picked it up. In a recording played on the podcast, he describes \"a piece of metal\" about two feet wide and two or three feet long that \"felt like you had nothing in your hands\" and \"wasn't any thicker than the foil out of a pack of cigarettes.\" Michels gives the second telling, from an interview by the journalist Bob Pratt. He credits it to Jesse Marcel Jr. and calls him the intelligence officer at the site, which differs from the first telling. In Michels's version, the foil-like debris did not burn when someone held a lighter to it. If the accounts are right, the wreckage was made of no material anyone knew."
